    category: "sustainability",
    excerpt:
      "Most cleaning cabinets hold a dozen bottles doing the work of three. A short argument for concentrates, refills and buying by task instead of by stain.",
    date: "2026-08-01",
    readingMinutes: 5,
    body: `<p>Open the cabinet under almost any kitchen sink and you find the same archaeology. A bottle bought for one stain three summers ago. Two half-empty sprays doing roughly the same job. Something in the back that nobody remembers purchasing. The clutter is not a failure of discipline. It is what happens when every cleaning problem is sold as its own product.</p>

<h2>Start with the shipping weight</h2>
<p>Most ready-to-use cleaners are mostly water. That water is manufactured into a bottle, trucked across the country, carried into your house, stored under your sink, and eventually thrown away in the container it came in. You are paying for the freight of something already plumbed into your kitchen.</p>
<p>Concentrated formats change the arithmetic. A small jar of fast-dissolving powder makes gallons of working solution, and the water it needs comes from the tap. Nothing about the routine changes: you mix, you apply, you wait, you wipe or drain. What changes is how much packaging passes through the house to get the same number of cleanings done.</p>

<h2>Count tasks, not bottles</h2>
<p>A more useful inventory question than "what do I own" is "what do I actually clean". Write down the real recurring jobs in your home. For most households the honest list is short: hard surfaces in the kitchen and bath, the toilet, floors, laundry, and one or two seasonal jobs like a tank or a set of outdoor furniture.</p>
<p>Now match products to that list, one per job, and let the rest go. A concentrated hard-surface cleaner covers counters, sinks and appliances. A drop-in <a href="/products/toilet-bomb-fragrance-free">toilet tablet</a> covers the bowl. A tank product handles the seasonal job. Three or four items, bought in the largest sensible size, will outperform a cabinet of eleven specialists, because you will actually reach for them.</p>

<h2>Read the line about what is left out</h2>
<p>When two products claim similar performance, the more informative half of the label is what is absent. Bleach-free, ammonia-free, alcohol-free, phosphate-free and biodegradable are concrete, checkable attributes rather than adjectives. They tell you what you are not storing in a cabinet, not carrying onto a boat, and not pouring down a drain.</p>
<p>They also tend to correlate with formats that travel well. Powders and tablets do not slosh, do not freeze, and do not need a spill tray in an RV bay or a galley locker.</p>

<h2>Buy on replenishment, not on impulse</h2>
<p>The bottles that pile up are almost always the ones bought reactively, in the middle of a mess, at whatever store was open. The ones that get used to the bottom are the ones that arrive on a rhythm you set in advance.</p>
<p>Pick the two or three consumables you genuinely repurchase, put them on a schedule that matches your actual consumption, and stop buying cleaning products in emergencies. A cabinet stocked this way stays roughly the same size year after year, which is the entire point.</p>

<h2>The test of a good cabinet</h2>
<p>You should be able to open the doors, see every item, name what each one is for, and know which one you will finish next. That is a low bar and almost no household clears it. Getting there is mostly subtraction.</p>
<p>If you want a starting point rather than a philosophy, our <a href="/collections/best-sellers">best sellers</a> are the products customers reorder most, and the <a href="/quiz">product quiz</a> will narrow a full cabinet down to a short list in about a minute. The rest of the library lives at <a href="/learn">Learn</a>.</p>`,
