    slug: "foliar-sprays-and-soil-drenches",
    title: "Foliar Sprays and Soil Drenches, Explained",
    category: "gardening",
    excerpt:
      "Two ways to apply a liquid inoculant, two different jobs. When to spray the leaf, when to drench the root zone, and how to time both.",
    date: "2026-06-24",
    readingMinutes: 6,
    body: `<p>A liquid inoculant comes with two sets of instructions on the same label, and gardeners often pick one at random. Foliar spray and soil drench are not interchangeable methods for delivering the same thing. They are different applications with different timing, different targets and different reasons to choose them.</p>

<h2>The soil drench</h2>
<p>A drench delivers diluted concentrate directly into the root zone, where the majority of the interesting activity in a garden happens. Roots do not absorb nutrients from soil in isolation. They trade with the microbial community around them, and that community does the work of converting locked-up nutrients into forms roots can actually take up.</p>
<p>A drench is the right choice when you are establishing something new: a freshly planted bed, a transplant settling in, a container refreshed with new media, or a bed coming out of dormancy in spring. It is also the correction when soil has been disturbed, because tilling, heavy rain and long dry spells all knock back the microbial population that took a season to build.</p>
<p>Apply enough volume to wet the root zone through, not just the surface. A light sprinkle over dry soil wets the top inch and evaporates by afternoon. Water the bed lightly first if it is bone dry, then drench.</p>

<h2>The foliar spray</h2>
<p>A foliar application coats the leaf surface itself. Leaves host their own microbial community, and a spray tops it up while also placing a very dilute nutrient solution where the plant can begin using it quickly.</p>
<p>Foliar work is the choice for maintenance rather than establishment: through the growing season, on plants pushing hard into fruit or flower, and after weather that has stripped the leaf surface. Coverage matters more than volume. Aim for a fine, even film on both the upper and lower leaf surfaces, and stop before it runs off. Liquid dripping onto the soil is a drench you did not mean to make.</p>

<h2>Timing is most of the technique</h2>
<p>Both applications go on in the early morning or the evening, never in strong midday sun. There are two reasons. Direct sun on wet leaves is hard on the plants and it evaporates the application before it does anything. And the living organisms you are applying do not appreciate heat and ultraviolet light any more than a seedling does.</p>
<p>Morning is slightly better than evening in humid climates, because leaves dry within a few hours rather than staying wet all night. In dry climates evening is fine and gives a longer contact time.</p>

<h2>Dilution, and the temptation to go stronger</h2>
<p><a href="/products/inoculant-liquid-concentrate">HVO Microbial Inoculant Concentrate</a> comes in 16 oz and 32 oz bottles and is designed to be diluted for either method. Follow the label rate. Concentrates are formulated at the strength the label describes, and doubling it does not double the result. In foliar work especially, a heavier mix mostly means more runoff.</p>
<p>Mix only what you will use in a session. A diluted living product is not a shelf-stable one, and a sprayer left half full in a shed for two weeks is worth emptying rather than applying.</p>

<h2>A season that uses both</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Early spring:</strong> drench beds as soil warms, before or just after planting.</li>
<li><strong>Late spring:</strong> drench each new transplant at planting, then leave it alone for two weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Summer:</strong> foliar spray every two to three weeks on actively growing crops, in the early morning.</li>
<li><strong>After heavy rain or a heat event:</strong> a foliar application once leaves have recovered.</li>
<li><strong>Autumn:</strong> a final drench as beds are put down for winter, alongside compost or an amendment.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where liquids fit in a larger system</h2>
<p>Liquid inoculant is a supplement to soil building, not a substitute for it. The dry amendments do the structural work over months, adding minerals and building the physical structure that holds moisture and air. The liquid keeps the biology topped up between those slower interventions, and reaches places a dry amendment cannot, such as established beds you would rather not disturb and containers already planted.</p>
<p>The rest of the range, including the dry bokashi products it pairs with, is in <a href="/collections/organic-gardening">Organic Gardening</a>.</p>`,
