      "How much water your roof can actually collect, what a complete catchment system needs, and the maintenance calendar to plan for before you buy a tank.",
    date: "2026-06-10",
    readingMinutes: 9,
    body: `<p>Rainwater catchment attracts people for very different reasons. Some want independence from a well that runs thin in August. Some are on an island or a ridge where a municipal connection was never on offer. Some simply object to irrigating a garden with treated water. Whatever brings you to it, the planning questions are the same, and they are more about arithmetic and maintenance than about equipment.</p>

<h2>Start with the roof, not the tank</h2>
<p>Your collection capacity is set by roof area and local rainfall, and neither is negotiable. The working figure is straightforward: one inch of rain falling on 1,000 square feet of roof yields roughly 600 gallons, before losses.</p>
<p>Measure the footprint of the roof, not its sloped surface. A pitched roof collects according to the area it shadows on the ground, because that is the area the rain falls through. Multiply that footprint by your annual rainfall in inches, then by 0.6, for a rough annual yield in gallons. Then take 15 to 20 percent off the top for what the system loses to first flush diversion, overflow during intense storms, splash and evaporation.</p>
<p>A 1,500 square foot footprint in a region receiving 40 inches a year works out to roughly 36,000 gallons gross, call it 30,000 usable. That number, divided across your actual demand, tells you whether you are building a garden supply or a whole-house system.</p>

<h2>Size the tank for the dry spell, not the year</h2>
<p>The most common sizing mistake is dividing annual yield by twelve. Rain does not arrive in monthly installments. What matters is your longest reliable dry period and your daily demand through it.</p>
<p>Take your average daily use, multiply by the length of your typical dry season in days, and add a margin. A household using 100 gallons a day through a 90 day dry season needs 9,000 gallons of storage to ride it out, regardless of how generous the wet season is. Storage is what converts a seasonal supply into a year-round one.</p>

<h2>What a complete system includes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gutters and downspouts</strong> sized for your heaviest rainfall intensity, not your average. Undersized gutters overflow exactly when collection matters most.</li>
<li><strong>Leaf screens or gutter guards</strong>, so the majority of debris never enters the system.</li>
<li><strong>A first flush diverter</strong> to send the initial, dirtiest volume of each storm away from the tank.</li>
<li><strong>An inlet screen</strong> on the tank itself, fine enough to exclude insects and debris.</li>
<li><strong>A calmed inlet</strong> that introduces water near the tank floor without stirring up settled sediment.</li>
<li><strong>An overflow</strong> sized to handle the full incoming flow, plumbed away from the foundation, and screened.</li>
<li><strong>A floating or elevated outlet</strong> that draws from clean mid-tank water rather than off the floor.</li>
<li><strong>An access hatch</strong> large enough to actually work through.</li>
</ul>
<p>That last item is worth insisting on before installation. A tank you cannot open is a tank you cannot maintain, and the difference in cost at purchase is trivial compared to the difference in ownership.</p>

<h2>Tank material, briefly</h2>
<p>Polyethylene is the default: inexpensive, light enough to place without a crane, available in every size, and easy to plumb. It must be opaque and it must be sited out of direct sun where possible. Ferrocement and poured concrete cost more and take longer, but they last decades and stay cooler, which matters in hot climates. Steel with a liner sits in between and suits large volumes.</p>
<p>Whatever the material, site it on a properly prepared, level base. A full 2,500 gallon tank weighs over ten tons. Bases fail slowly and expensively.</p>

<h2>The maintenance calendar</h2>
<p>Plan this before you buy, because it is the part that determines whether the system is a pleasure or a chore.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monthly in the wet season:</strong> check gutters and inlet screens, empty the first flush chamber.</li>
<li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> inspect the tank interior through the hatch, check the overflow screen, note the sediment level.</li>
<li><strong>Twice a year:</strong> clean the catchment surface and the gutter runs.</li>
<li><strong>Annually or as needed:</strong> a full tank cleaning, timed for when the tank is at its seasonal low.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the annual job, a fast-dissolving tank product is far less work than draining and scrubbing by hand. <a href="/products/cistern-catchment-bomb">Cistern &amp; Catchment Bomb</a> deep cleans tank walls and interior surfaces through Powerful Oxidation, breaking down contaminant residue and buildup, cleaning mildew stains and algae stains, and neutralizing odors at the source. It is bleach-free, ammonia-free, phosphate-free and biodegradable.</p>
<p>This is a cleaning product for tank walls and surfaces, for use in non-potable water systems.</p>

<h2>The honest summary</h2>
<p>Catchment is not free water. It is a system you own and maintain in exchange for a supply you control. The households that stay happy with it are the ones that sized the tank for the dry season, built in an access hatch they can work through, and put four maintenance dates in the calendar on day one.</p>
<p>Products for the whole system are in <a href="/collections/cistern-catchment">Cistern &amp; Catchment</a>, and the rest of the library is at <a href="/learn">Learn</a>.</p>`,
