    slug: "when-to-change-a-water-filter",
    title: "When to Change a Water Filter, and How to Tell",
    category: "water",
    excerpt:
      "Filter life is printed as a range for a reason. Four signals that tell you where you actually fall inside it, and how to stop guessing.",
    date: "2026-05-14",
    readingMinutes: 5,
    body: `<p>Every filter carries a rating and almost nobody follows it exactly. The rating is not the manufacturer being vague. It is an honest acknowledgement that filter life depends on inputs the manufacturer cannot see: how much water you run, how hard it is, and what is in it before it arrives.</p>

<h2>Why the number is a range</h2>
<p>Life MXL replacement filters are rated for 6 to 12 months. That factor of two is the difference between a two-person household on soft municipal water and a busy family on hard well water running the machine several times a day. Both are using the filter correctly. They will simply reach the end of it at different times.</p>
<p>The same logic applies to pitchers. A <a href="/products/pitcher-of-life-2nd-generation">Pitcher of Life</a> cartridge sees a very different workload in a household filling it twice a day than in one filling it twice a week.</p>

<h2>Signal one: flow rate</h2>
<p>The most reliable indicator, and the easiest to miss, because it changes gradually. A filter approaching the end of its service life passes water more slowly as the media loads up.</p>
<p>Give yourself a baseline you can compare against. The week you install a new filter, time how long a full pitcher takes to draw through, or how long it takes to fill a specific glass at the ionizer tap. Write it on a piece of tape inside the cabinet. When that time has roughly doubled, you have your answer without any guesswork.</p>

<h2>Signal two: taste returning</h2>
<p>Filtration media does not stop working all at once, it stops working incrementally, and taste tracks that decline closely. Chlorine taste is usually the first thing to come back, because it is the first thing most media handles.</p>
<p>The catch is that your palate adapts as the change happens slowly. The useful test is comparative rather than absolute: pour a glass of filtered water and a glass straight from the tap, let both come to the same temperature, and taste them side by side. Early in a filter's life the difference is obvious. When you have to concentrate to find it, the filter is telling you something.</p>

<h2>Signal three: the calendar</h2>
<p>For anything you cannot easily observe, the calendar wins. Set a recurring reminder at the conservative end of the range rather than the generous one, and move it later only if flow and taste say you can.</p>
<p>Buying in multiples helps here more than people expect. A single spare filter in a drawer is the difference between changing it on the day you notice and changing it in three weeks when you get around to ordering. <a href="/products/pitcher-of-life-replacement-filter-3pack">Three-packs</a> exist for this reason.</p>

<h2>Signal four: a change upstream</h2>
<p>Anything that alters what enters the filter resets the clock. Municipal work on the mains, a switch in seasonal source, new plumbing in the house, or a well that has been drawn hard through a dry summer will all load a filter faster than usual. If your water looked or tasted different for a few days, assume the filter absorbed that difference and shorten the interval accordingly.</p>

<h2>Two small habits</h2>
<p>Date the filter, not the box. A strip of tape with the install date on the housing survives every move, every distraction and every change of mind about when you last did it.</p>
<p>And flush a new filter properly on installation, following the instructions that came with it. The first draw through fresh media is not representative, and skipping the flush is the most common reason a brand new filter tastes worse than the one it replaced.</p>
<p>Replacements for both systems live in <a href="/collections/water-filtration">Water Filtration</a>, including the <a href="/products/life-mxl-replacement-filter">Life MXL replacement filter</a>.</p>`,
